{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974360-sleepwalkers-and-breaking-points-2e","id":974360,"title":"Sleepwalkers and breaking points (2E)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-09-22T06:10:01-07:00","id":974360,"title":"Sleepwalkers and breaking points (2E)","content":"So, I'm confused about something. Chronicles of Darkness core as well as the precedent set by the first edition of Mage the Awakening seem to conflict with what Mage the Awakening second edition is saying. Specifically, regarding sleepwalkers and integrity. Integrity being possessed by banishers is explicitly the reason why they go insane when they witness magic. Simple, right? Except for the fact that Sleepwalkers in first edition had no such troubles despite them possessing integrity all the same. This is further complicated by the rules for dissonance, which state that only on a dramatic failure by the sleeper do they suffer a breaking point, even when the sidebar directly to the right states that witnessing magic is /always/ a breaking point. So either sleepers always suffer one and have the potential to suffer two breaking points in tandem with sleepwalkers always suffering one and being as crazy as banishers... Or this is a mistake, and the Chronicles of Darkness core indicating that breaking points are that which shake the character's understanding of reality as well as the implication of dramatically failing a dissonance roll indicate that sleepwalkers do not suffer breaking points from this, and sleepers only optionally suffer breaking points.<br /> <br /> \u200bThoughts? And the sidebar/rules I'm referring to are on page 299 of 2E's core.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2532-blitzkitty","name":"BlitzKitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2532_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T06:29:14-07:00","id":974368,"title":"","content":"Those are two separate mechanics, and both are true.<br /> <br /> First, when a Sleeper witness and obvious spell, he suffers a Breaking Point and has to check for a potential Integrity loss. The player or ST rolls Resolve + Composure minus any modifier, just like any other Breaking Point.<br /> <br /> Secondly, at the end of the scene, the Sleeper also has to roll for Dissonance. The player or ST rolls his unmodified Integrity rating as a dice pool to see if the spell gets degraded. If the roll is a dramatic failure, however, the Sleeper suffers <i>another</i> Breaking Point.<br /> <br /> I hope this helps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T08:03:10-07:00","id":974418,"title":"","content":"What zooroos said","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T08:10:11-07:00","id":974422,"title":"","content":"So, as Integrity can't go to zero, and the roll for Dissonance requires the unmodified Integrity score, does that mean only PCs that chose to make a failure into a dramatic failure can ever get that additional Dissonance Breaking Points?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/158192-what-s-your-custom-legacy?p=1109906#post1109906\" target=\"_blank\">My custom legacy</a> (2e)- The Disciples of Rathma - Life/Death focused Moros/Thyrsus Legacy, comments appreciated","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/166-joker","name":"Joker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"477"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T09:04:11-07:00","id":974449,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Joker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974360-sleepwalkers-and-breaking-points-2e?p=974422#post974422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, as Integrity can't go to zero, and the roll for Dissonance requires the unmodified Integrity score, does that mean only PCs that chose to make a failure into a dramatic failure can ever get that additional Dissonance Breaking Points?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the Sleeper <i>could</i> be currently afflicted with something that reduces their Integrity to 0, at which point they'd roll a chance die. That said, that seems like a fairly rare thing to happen. Is the Integrity roll ever modified by anything?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T09:11:13-07:00","id":974454,"title":"","content":"Actually, it's also withstood by the spell's potency so that could reduce the dicepool to zero. Furthermore, I was more concerned about the fact that /Sleepwalkers/ have Integrity, which would be adversely affected by magic. Seems like they'd be kinda screwed over in the company of mages, unlike in first edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2532-blitzkitty","name":"BlitzKitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2532_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T09:17:59-07:00","id":974459,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlitzKitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974360-sleepwalkers-and-breaking-points-2e?p=974454#post974454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, it's also withstood by the spell's potency so that could reduce the dicepool to zero. Furthermore, I was more concerned about the fact that /Sleepwalkers/ have Integrity, which would be adversely affected by magic. Seems like they'd be kinda screwed over in the company of mages, unlike in first edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really. While Sleepwalkers have Integrity, they're the exception to all the negative aspects of witnessing Supernal Magic. Sleepwalkers neither roll for a Breaking Point from witnessing obvious magic nor do they ever roll for Dissonance, so they can never Dramatically Fail a Dissonance roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T09:25:49-07:00","id":974463,"title":"","content":"Sleepwalkers are fine since their merit explicitly grants them immunity to breaking points caused by Supernal magic.<br /> <br /> Banishers by the way can have Wisdom if they turn after a normal Awakening. The ones who kept Integrity were basically Sleepers who failed to make a full transition which is why they're stuck between the powers of a Mage and the trauma of a Sleeper. Sleepwalkers who Awaken to being a Banisher could go either way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-22T13:00:40-07:00","id":974616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BlitzKitty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/974360-sleepwalkers-and-breaking-points-2e?p=974454#post974454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, it's also withstood by the spell's potency so that could reduce the dicepool to zero. Furthermore, I was more concerned about the fact that /Sleepwalkers/ have Integrity, which would be adversely affected by magic. Seems like they'd be kinda screwed over in the company of mages, unlike in first edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>One of the functions of the Sleepwalker Merit is that witnessing supernal magic doesn't count as an Integrity Breaking Point for the character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-23T04:35:53-07:00","id":974988,"title":"","content":"As others have said, and in response to the OP, Sleepwalkers use the Integrity rules, with the exception that they don't suffer Breaking points for witnessing magic, don't provoke Dissonance nor increase Paradox risk.<br /> <br /> A whole different thing is the subject of Awakening as a Banisher. A mortal doesn't need to become a Sleepwalker in order to Awake, they can go straight from Sleeper to Awakened. Also, not all Banishers Awake with an Integrity trait. The term 'Banisher' is an umbrella term, more like a social label than a supernatural condition. There are Banishers who were well-adjusted members of their respective magical societies before turning against their own kind.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}